WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.900
One Ukrainian officer is on trial in Hamburg over blasts that cut Europe's Russian gas. This is Omni View Top World News, episode 22. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. Hamburg opened that case on Wednesday. The Guardian names the defendant as Special Forces Officer, Serhi Kuznetsov. German privacy rules also list him as Serhi K. Prosecutors say he helped plan a secret attack on civilian pipes, and they say that would be a war crime. He denies it.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.800
So does Ukraine's government, and so does President Vladimir Zelensky.

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:38.440
The charge sheet described in court talks about at least four devices, each up to 27 kilograms,

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:40.860
about 80 meters down near Bornholm.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:46.180
Six other people are alleged to have helped, among them divers, and explosives expert and

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.600
a captain, using a rented yacht and timers.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.280
He walked in and saluted.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:55.620
That sits badly in Berlin, because Germany is Ukraine's largest European supplier of military

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.140
aid.

00:00:56.140 --> 00:01:01.060
Moscow, which denies blowing up its own lines, said on Monday it wants a verdict that

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.200
names the bombers and the people who ordered them.

00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:07.300
Before the war, half of Germany's gas imports came through Nord Stream 1.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:12.060
A second line was already built, and Berlin cancelled the permit to open it just before

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:14.180
Russia's February 2022 invasion.

00:01:14.740 --> 00:01:20.720
Another man, Volodymyr Zhirovlyov, also called Vladimir Z, was arrested in Croatia and is

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:21.680
fighting extradition.

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:24.620
The Hamburg hearings are expected to run into February.

00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:29.660
Saudi Arabia's Supreme Court has lifted a death sentence on a Facebook comment.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.060
Deputy Foreign Minister Arun Hemachandra of Sri Lanka said so, in an account carried by

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:35.560
the BBC.

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.040
The man is a no-john Sivirasa, 27, a Sri Lankan migrant worker, an appeal court sentenced

00:01:42.040 --> 00:01:46.580
him to death in September after convicting him of blasphemy against Islam and the Prophet

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:47.000
Muhammad.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:52.000
Hemachandra said Wednesdays ruling set that sentence aside, and he thanked legal teams

00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:52.840
in both countries.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:59.080
The comment came in late June, after a clip spread of a young Muslim man kicking a dog near Hindu pilgrims.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:04.580
Sivirasa answered in Tamil with an insult to the prophet, deleted it, and posted an apology.

00:02:05.140 --> 00:02:09.100
A screenshot reached his employer in Saudi officials. He was arrested in July.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:14.420
A lower court in Al-Asa had first given him five years and a fine of three million Saudi reels.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.620
Lawmaker Fizer Mustafa told the BBC the death sentence is gone, and lawyers are still reading the new ruling.

00:02:20.620 --> 00:02:24.320
It is not clear if he stays detained, or if another case follows.

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.500
His family says they have not spoken to him directly since the 28th of September.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.440
Joey Shea of Human Rights Watch said Tuesday that Saudi authorities should stop using harsh

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:37.240
speech laws for extreme sentences, especially against migrant workers.

